  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1026 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nFor late tonight thru Fri morning main concern is how widespread \ndense fog may become along portions of the I-25 Corridor from \nDenver north to the Wyoming border and points eastward. HRRR has \nbeen consistent in showing fog developing over ern Larimer and \nWeld county by midnight and then spreading it southward into nrn \nportions of the Denver area by 3 am or 4 am. This fog would then \nlinger thru the mid morning hours on Fri before lifting. In \naddition, soundings show some potential for drizzle as well.\n\nMeanwhile, for the rest of Fri, the flow aloft will be SW with \nonly isold showers possible in the mtns and far nern plains in the\naftn. As for highs, readings will be in the lower to mid 60's \nacross the plains, however, lingering low clouds could keep temps \nin the 50's over the far nern plains.\n\nFor Sat, little change is expected as SW flow aloft remains over the \narea. There still appears to be a weak disturbance embedded in \nthe flow which may bring a chc of showers to portions of the \nhigher terrain with an isold shower threat across the plains. \nTemperatures will remain in the upper 50's to mid 60's over nern \nCO.\n\nLooking ahead to Sun and Sun night, there continues to be some \nuncertainty as to how an upper level trough along the West Coast \nevolves.  The ECMWF and GFS now have a disorganized elongated upper \nlevel trough moving into the nrn Rockies and Great Basin by Sun \nnight.  Ahead of this feature, there will be quite a bit of \nsubtropical moisture affecting the higher terrain, especially by Sun \nnight.  As a result, should see an increasing chc of showers over \nthe higher terrain with snow levels dropping below 10000 ft late Sun \nnight.  Across the plains, it looks dry and windy thru Sun aftn as \nsfc low pres intensifies from ern WY into ern CO.  By Sun night, \nlatest data shows varying precip chances across the plains. \nFor now, have kept in a chc of showers as a band of mid level QG \nascent moves across overnight.\n\nOn Mon, the ECMWF and GFS have two main areas of energy associated \nwith the elongated upper level trough.  One piece stays over the nrn \nRockies while a 2nd piece of energy moves across srn CO/nrn NM.  If \nthis occurs best chc of decent precip would stay mainly to the north \nand south of the area.  Meanwhile, both models shows a stg cold \nfront moving across the plains with gusty north winds behind it. \n  \nFor Mon night into Tue, the ECMWF and GFS keep the two pieces of \nenergy separated as the one piece over the nrn Rockies moves SSE \nacross Colorado while the other piece of energy moves from nern NM \ninto central Oklahoma. In addition, the GFS intensifies the upper\nlevel low as it moves into Oklahoma by late Mon night. The ECMWF \nhas very little additional precip Mon night into Tue as much drier\nair spreads across the area. Meanwhile, the GFS has a band of \nprecip over the ern plains Mon night but also decreases the chc of\nprecip over the area on Tue. Winds will remain gusty over the \nplains Mon night into early Tue as well.\n  \nBy Wed an upper level ridge will build over the area ahead of \nanother possible system moving across on Thu. For now, Wed looks \ndry and warmer ahead of the next system.",
